## A VPN and antivirus that run quietly on every computer and phone in the house, and on the road.

My entire family runs on a single Surfshark account. The VPN and the antivirus are on every computer in the house and on our Android phones. We’ve been on Surfshark One since June 2024, and in that time I’ve personally used the VPN in six countries, and it has been flawless in every one.

I don’t hand out five stars easily. This one earned it.

### Why a VPN Matters When You Travel

When you’re on hotel Wi-Fi, airport Wi-Fi, or a foreign cell network, you have no idea who runs that network or what they log. A VPN wraps everything your phone or laptop sends in encryption and routes it through Surfshark’s server. Whoever operates the network you’re on sees encrypted traffic directed to a single destination. They don’t see where you’re going or what you’re doing.

That’s the protection you want when you check a bank balance from a café or answer email from a hotel room. It’s also why the VPN stays on at home.

### One Account for Everyone

This is what sold us. Surfshark doesn’t limit how many devices can use the VPN, so one subscription covers the whole family. Nobody has to decide whose phone gets protected. Everyone signs in with the same account, and every device is covered.

We run the antivirus on eight devices. If you’re signing up for the first time, Surfshark’s antivirus page currently lists protection for up to five devices per account on Windows, Mac, and Android, so plan around that.

### Six Countries, No Drama

I’ve used it in six countries on every kind of connection: hotel Wi-Fi, public hotspots and foreign cell signals. It has been flawless everywhere I’ve taken it. Surfshark runs more than 4,500 servers in 100 countries, so there’s almost always one close by.

The app is simple. Open it, tap connect, done. You don’t need to know anything about networking to use it, which matters when the whole family is on it.

### The Antivirus

Surfshark One adds a real antivirus to the VPN, in the same app. It scans in real time, runs scheduled scans, and updates its threat database automatically. It runs on Windows 10 and later, macOS Big Sur 11 and later, and Android 10 and later. It doesn’t run on Windows computers with ARM processors or on Android devices that aren’t 64-bit, so check your hardware before you count on it. One more limitation: its web protection, which blocks dangerous sites, only works on Windows right now.

### Can You Trust a VPN Company?

A VPN sees all of your traffic, so the company behind it matters more than the app. Here’s what Surfshark has done to back up its claims:

- **Audited no-logs policy.** Deloitte, one of the Big Four accounting firms, checked Surfshark’s no-logs policy in 2023 and again in 2025.
- **RAM-only servers.** Every Surfshark server runs from memory instead of a hard drive, so data is wiped whenever a server restarts.
- **Where it’s based.** Surfshark is based in the Netherlands, which is part of the Nine Eyes intelligence-sharing alliance. If a company keeps no logs, there’s nothing to hand over, but you should know where it sits.

### What It Costs, and the Black Friday Deal

Surfshark is running its Black Friday sale right now. Surfshark One, the plan with the VPN and the antivirus, is $2.49 a month on the two-year plan, with 3 extra months free. That’s the same price as the Starter plan, which has no antivirus. You pay $67.23 up front for the first 27 months.

Surfshark hasn’t announced when the sale ends. After the first term, the plan renews at its regular yearly rate, about $99 a year for Surfshark One as of this writing. Every plan comes with a 30-day money-back guarantee.

For a whole family’s VPN plus antivirus, that’s less than a lot of people pay for one standalone antivirus license.

### What I’d Change

- **The renewal price.** The starting price is a promotion. Put the renewal date on your calendar.
- **The antivirus limits.** New plans list antivirus for up to five devices, and there’s no iPhone version.

### THE_VERDICT

Surfshark does exactly what I pay it to do. It keeps my family’s phones and computers private and protected, at home and in six countries so far, on networks I’d never trust on their own. It’s simple enough that nobody in the family has to think about it. Five stars, and we’ll keep renewing it.

